<p>
  Bark anoles come from Hispaniola, the island shared by Haiti and the Dominican
  Republic, and in Miami they specialize on one thing: wide tree trunks. Where
  most anoles perch on narrower branches, bark anoles press themselves flat against
  broad bark between one and three meters up, which gives them their slightly
  splayed look.
</p>
<p>
  They are the smallest anoles in Miami, no longer than about 5 cm from snout to
  vent, and very fast. Walk up to a tree and a bark anole will dash to the
  far side of the trunk before you get a good look, a habit field biologists call
  &ldquo;squirreling.&rdquo; Their favorite food is ants.
</p>

<h2>Life on the trunk</h2>
<p>
  On Lizard Island, our long-term study site in Miami, the bark anole is the
  community's trunk specialist, living between the ground-dwelling brown and
  crested anoles below and the green and knight anoles in the canopy above. Being
  in the middle has a cost. Knight anoles eat other lizards, and in August 2013 we
  watched an adult female knight anole on the Florida International University
  campus swallow a juvenile bark anole in under a minute.
</p>
<p>
  Bark anoles were also one of six species whose cold tolerance shifted after
  south Florida's extreme cold snap in January 2020; see our
  <a href="/research/cold-snaps/">cold snaps page</a>.
</p>
